{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789824337480,"sessionID":"ses_f46293753ffeU5Yf7iVUgOE0mm","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9d8059d001wl6VDVS2i08nnT","sessionID":"ses_f46293753ffeU5Yf7iVUgOE0mm","messageID":"msg_0b9d7fb72001hZRj28iv4D8Umk","type":"tool","id":"call_00_jquCvLPthD8xptml8Xur6526","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":300,"offset":767,"path":"/tmp/opencode/filing_pages.txt"},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/filing_pages.txt, lines 767-1066\n767: <<<PAGE 19 folio=13>>>\n768: Table of Contents\n769: QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS ABOUT THE MERGER\n770: AND THE SPECIAL MEETING\n771: The following questions and answers address briefly some questions you may have regarding the merger agreement,\n772: the merger and the special meeting. These questions and answers may not address all questions that may be important to you as a holder of shares of Mac-Gray common stock. For important additional\n773: information, please refer to the more detailed discussion contained elsewhere in this proxy statement, the annexes to this proxy statement and the documents referred to in this proxy statement,\n774: including the merger agreement.\n775: Q:\n776: What is the effect of the merger?\n777: A:\n778: Mac-Gray,\n779: the Buyer Entities and Merger Subsidiary have entered into the merger agreement. Subject to the terms and conditions of the merger agreement,\n780: Parent will acquire Mac-Gray through the merger of Merger Subsidiary, a wholly-owned subsidiary of Parent, with and into Mac-Gray. Mac-Gray will be the surviving corporation in the merger and will\n781: continue as a wholly-owned subsidiary of Parent.\n782: Q:\n783: What will happen to my Mac-Gray stock as a result of the merger?\n784: A:\n785: If\n786: the merger is completed, each share of Mac-Gray common stock that you hold at the effective time of the merger will be converted into the right to receive\n787: $21.25 in cash, without interest, less any applicable withholding taxes. This conversion does not apply to shares of Mac-Gray common stock held by any Mac-Gray stockholders who have properly\n788: perfected, and not withdrawn or lost, their appraisal rights under Delaware law as more fully described below. The closing sale price of a share of Mac-Gray common stock on the New York Stock Exchange\n789: on November 27, 2013 was $21.30. You are encouraged to obtain current market quotations for Mac-Gray common stock in connection with voting your shares.\n790: Q:\n791: What will happen to Mac-Gray generally as a result of the merger?\n792: A:\n793: If\n794: the merger is completed, Mac-Gray will cease to be an independent public company and will be wholly owned by Parent. As a result, you will no longer have\n795: any ownership interest in Mac-Gray. Upon completion of the merger, shares of Mac-Gray common stock will no longer be listed on the New York Stock Exchange, or any other stock exchange or quotation\n796: system. In addition, following the completion of the merger, the registration of Mac-Gray common stock and our reporting obligations under the Exchange Act will be terminated and there will be no\n797: further public market for shares of Mac-Gray common stock.\n798: Q:\n799: How does the merger consideration compare to the market price of Mac-Gray common\n800: stock?\n801: A:\n802: The\n803: merger consideration of $21.25 per share to be received by Mac-Gray stockholders represents:\n804: •\n805: an approximately 36% premium over the high of $15.61 per share for the 52-week period ending October 11, 2013; and\n806: •\n807: an approximately 44% premium to the $14.72 closing price of our common stock on October 11, 2013 (the last trading\n808: day prior to the date our Board of Directors approved the merger agreement).\n809: The\n810: closing sale price of a share of Mac-Gray common stock on the New York Stock Exchange on November 27, 2013 was $21.30. You are encouraged to obtain current market quotations for Mac-Gray\n811: common stock in connection with voting your shares.\n812: 13\n813: \n814: <<<PAGE 20 folio=14>>>\n815: Table of Contents\n816: Q:\n817: When do you expect the merger to be completed?\n818: A:\n819: We\n820: currently expect the merger to be completed in January 2014. However, the merger is subject to various closing conditions, including Mac-Gray stockholder\n821: and regulatory approvals, and it is possible that the failure to timely meet these closing conditions or other factors outside of our control could delay the completion of the merger. We cannot assure\n822: you that we will complete the merger on this schedule or at all.\n823: Q:\n824: Why am I receiving this proxy statement?\n825: A:\n826: You\n827: are receiving this proxy statement because you were a stockholder of Mac-Gray as of November 27, 2013, the record date for the special meeting.\n828: The adoption of the merger agreement requires the affirmative vote of a majority of the outstanding shares of Mac-Gray common stock entitled to vote at the special meeting. A copy of the merger\n829: agreement is attached to this proxy statement as Annex A. Mac-Gray will submit the merger agreement to its stockholders for adoption at the special meeting described in this proxy statement.\n830: You should read the section entitled \"The Special Meeting\" beginning on page 20 of this proxy statement.\n831: Q:\n832: When and where will the special meeting of stockholders be held?\n833: A:\n834: The\n835: special meeting of Mac-Gray stockholders will be held on Wednesday, January 8, 2014 at 10:00 a.m., local time, at the Goodwin Procter LLP Conference Center, Second Floor, Exchange Place, 53 State\n836: Street, Boston, Massachusetts 02109.\n837: Q:\n838: What are the proposals that will be voted on at the special meeting?\n839: A:\n840: You\n841: will be asked to consider and vote on (1) a proposal to adopt the merger agreement with the Buyer Entities and Merger Subsidiary, (2) an\n842: advisory (non-binding) proposal to approve the \"golden parachute\" compensation payable or that could become payable to the named executive officers of Mac-Gray in connection with the merger pursuant\n843: to the terms of the merger agreement and pre-existing severance arrangements and (3) a proposal to approve one or more adjournments or postponements of the special meeting, if necessary, to\n844: solicit additional proxies if Mac-Gray has not obtained sufficient affirmative stockholder votes to adopt the merger agreement.\n845: Q:\n846: How does the Mac-Gray Board of Directors recommend that I vote on the proposals?\n847: A:\n848: The\n849: Mac-Gray Board of Directors (upon the unanimous recommendation of the Special Committee) unanimously approved the merger agreement and determined that\n850: the merger and the other transactions contemplated by the merger agreement are advisable and in the best interests of Mac-Gray and our stockholders and unanimously recommends that you vote\n851: \" FOR \" the proposal to adopt the merger agreement. You should read the section entitled \"The Merger—Reasons for the Merger and Recommendation\n852: of our Board of Directors\" beginning on page 42 of this proxy statement. The Mac-Gray Board of Directors also unanimously recommends that you vote \" FOR \"\n853: the proposal to approve, on an advisory (non-binding) basis, the \"golden parachute\" compensation payable or that could become payable to the named executive officers of Mac-Gray in connection with the\n854: merger the terms of the merger agreement and pursuant to pre-existing severance arrangements and \" FOR \" the adjournment proposal.\n855: Q:\n856: Why am I being asked to cast an advisory (non-binding) vote to approve the \"golden parachute\" compensation payable or that could\n857: become payable to certain Mac-Gray officers in connection with the merger pursuant to the terms of the merger agreement and pre-existing severance\n858: arrangements?\n859: A:\n860: The\n861: SEC, in accordance with the Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act of 2010, adopted rules that require Mac-Gray to seek an advisory\n862: (non-binding) vote with respect to certain payments that may be made to Mac-Gray's named executive officers in connection with the merger.\n863: 14\n864: \n865: <<<PAGE 21 folio=15>>>\n866: Table of Contents\n867: Q:\n868: What will happen if Mac-Gray shareholders do not approve the \"golden parachute\" compensation at the special\n869: meeting?\n870: A:\n871: Approval\n872: of the \"golden parachute\" compensation payable or that could become payable to Mac-Gray's named executive officers in connection with the merger\n873: pursuant to the terms of the merger agreement and pre-existing severance arrangements is not a condition to completion of the merger. The vote with respect to the \"golden parachute\" compensation is an\n874: advisory vote and will not be binding on Mac-Gray regardless of whether the merger agreement is approved. Therefore, regardless of whether stockholders approve the \"golden parachute\" compensation, if\n875: the merger is approved by the stockholders and completed, the \"golden parachute\" compensation will still be paid to Mac-Gray's named executive officers to the extent payable in accordance with the\n876: terms of the merger agreement and pre-existing compensation arrangements.\n877: Q:\n878: What vote of Mac-Gray stockholders is required to adopt the proposal regarding \"golden parachute\" compensation and the proposal to\n879: adjourn the special meeting, if necessary?\n880: A:\n881: A\n882: majority of the votes properly cast will be required to approve the advisory (non-binding) vote on the \"golden parachute\" compensation payable or that\n883: could become payable to the named executive officers of Mac-Gray in connection with the merger and the adjournment, if necessary, of the special meeting to solicit additional proxies in favor of the\n884: approval and adoption of the merger agreement. Because the \"golden parachute\" compensation proposal is advisory, it will not be binding upon the Mac-Gray Board of Directors regardless of whether the\n885: merger agreement is approved. Failure to vote, in person or by proxy, will have no effect on the approval of the \"golden parachute\" compensation or any adjournment proposal.\n886: Q:\n887: Who is entitled to attend and vote at the special meeting?\n888: A:\n889: The\n890: record date for the special meeting is November 27, 2013. If you own shares of Mac-Gray common stock as of the close of business on the record\n891: date, you are entitled to notice of, and to vote at, the special meeting or any adjournment or postponement of the special meeting. As of the record date, there were approximately 14,758,306 shares of\n892: Mac-Gray common stock issued and outstanding held collectively by approximately 104 stockholders of record.\n893: Q:\n894: How many votes are required to adopt the merger agreement?\n895: A:\n896: Under\n897: applicable law, we must receive the affirmative approval of the holders of a majority of the outstanding shares of Mac-Gray common stock entitled to\n898: vote at the special meeting in order to complete the merger. In connection with the execution of the merger agreement, certain stockholders of Mac-Gray entered into voting agreements agreeing to vote\n899: in favor of the adoption of the merger agreement. As of November 27, 2013, the record date for the special meeting, these stockholders collectively owned approximately 15% of the Mac-Gray\n900: common stock entitled to vote at the special meeting. If the merger agreement is terminated in accordance with its terms, these voting agreements will also terminate.\n901: Q:\n902: How are votes counted?\n903: A:\n904: Votes\n905: will be counted by the inspector of election appointed for the special meeting, who will separately count \"FOR\" and \"AGAINST\" votes and abstentions.\n906: The adoption of the merger agreement requires the affirmative vote of a majority of the outstanding shares of Mac-Gray common stock entitled to vote at the special meeting. If you abstain from voting,\n907: fail to cast your vote, in person or by proxy, or fail to give voting instructions to your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee, it will have the same effect as a vote against the proposal to\n908: adopt the merger agreement.\n909: 15\n910: \n911: <<<PAGE 22 folio=16>>>\n912: Table of Contents\n913: Q:\n914: What do I need to do now?\n915: A:\n916: After\n917: carefully reading and considering the information contained in this proxy statement, including the annexes and the other documents referred to in this\n918: proxy statement, please vote your shares as described below. You have one vote for each share of Mac-Gray common stock you own as of the record date.\n919: Q:\n920: How do I vote if I am a stockholder of record?\n921: A:\n922: You\n923: may vote:\n924: •\n925: by telephone by following the voting instructions printed on your proxy card;\n926: •\n927: by Internet by following the voting instructions printed on your proxy card;\n928: •\n929: by completing, signing and dating each proxy card you receive and returning it in the enclosed postage paid envelope; or\n930: •\n931: in person by appearing and casting your vote at the special meeting.\n932: If\n933: you are voting by telephone or via the Internet, your voting instructions must be received by the date and time indicated on the applicable proxy card(s).\n934: Voting\n935: via the Internet, by telephone or by mailing in your proxy card will not prevent you from voting in person at the special meeting. You are encouraged to submit a proxy by mail, via the Internet\n936: or by telephone even if you plan to attend the special meeting in person, to ensure that your shares of Mac-Gray common stock are present in person or represented at the special meeting.\n937: If\n938: you return a properly signed and dated proxy card but do not mark the box showing how you wish to vote, your shares will be voted \" FOR \" the proposal\n939: to adopt the merger agreement, \" FOR \" the proposal to approve, on an advisory (non-binding) basis, the \"golden parachute\" compensation payable or that\n940: could become payable to the named executive officers of Mac-Gray in connection with the merger pursuant to the terms of the merger agreement and pre-existing severance arrangements and\n941: \" FOR \" the proposal to adjourn the special meeting. With respect to any other matter that properly comes before the special meeting, shares present in\n942: person or represented by all proxies received by Mac-Gray will be voted with respect to such matter in accordance with the judgment of the persons named as attorneys-in-fact in the proxies.\n943: Q:\n944: How do I vote if my shares are held by my brokerage firm, bank, trust or other\n945: nominee?\n946: A:\n947: If\n948: your shares are held in a brokerage account or by another nominee, such as a bank or trust, then the brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee is\n949: considered to be the stockholder of record with respect to those shares. However, you still are considered to be the beneficial owner of those shares, with your shares being held in \"street name.\"\n950: \"Street name\" holders generally cannot vote their shares directly and must instead instruct the brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee how to vote their shares. Your brokerage firm, bank, trust\n951: or other nominee will only be permitted to vote your shares for you at the special meeting if you instruct it how to vote. Therefore, it is important that you promptly follow the directions provided\n952: by your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee regarding how to instruct them to vote your shares. If you wish to vote in person at the special meeting, you must bring a proxy from your\n953: brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee authorizing you to vote at the special meeting.\n954: In\n955: addition, because any shares you may hold in \"street name\" will be deemed to be held by a different stockholder than any shares you hold of record, shares held in \"street name\" will not be combined\n956: for voting purposes with shares you hold of record. To be sure your shares are voted,\n957: 16\n958: \n959: <<<PAGE 23 folio=17>>>\n960: Table of Contents\n961: you\n962: should instruct your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee to vote your shares. Shares held by a corporation or business entity must be voted by an authorized officer of the entity.\n963: Q:\n964: What does it mean if I receive more than one proxy card?\n965: A:\n966: If\n967: you receive more than one proxy card, it means that you hold shares that are registered in more than one account. For example, if you own your shares in\n968: various registered forms, such as jointly with your spouse, as trustee of a trust or as custodian for a minor, you will receive, and you will need to sign and return, a separate proxy card for those\n969: shares because they are held in a different form of record ownership. Therefore, to ensure that all of your shares are voted, you will need to sign and return each proxy card you receive or vote by\n970: telephone or via the Internet by using the different control number(s) on each proxy card.\n971: Q:\n972: May I change my vote after I have delivered my proxy?\n973: A:\n974: Yes.\n975: If you are the stockholder of record of Mac-Gray common stock, you have the right to change or revoke your proxy at any time before the vote being taken\n976: at the special meeting:\n977: •\n978: by delivering to Mac-Gray's corporate secretary a signed written notice of revocation bearing a date later than the date\n979: of the proxy, stating that the proxy is revoked;\n980: •\n981: by attending the special meeting and voting in person (your attendance at the meeting will not, by itself, revoke your\n982: proxy; you must vote in person at the meeting);\n983: •\n984: by signing and delivering a new proxy relating to the same shares of Mac-Gray common stock and bearing a later date; or\n985: •\n986: by submitting another proxy by telephone or via the Internet by the date and time indicated on the applicable proxy\n987: card(s).\n988: The\n989: latest dated completed proxy will be the one which counts. Written notices of revocation and other communications with respect to the revocation of any proxies should be addressed to:\n990: Mac-Gray\n991: Corporation\n992: 404 Wyman Street, Suite 400\n993: Waltham, Massachusetts 02451\n994: Attn: Corporate Secretary\n995: If\n996: you are a \"street name\" holder of Mac-Gray common stock, you should contact your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee to obtain instructions as to how to change or revoke your proxy.\n997: Q:\n998: What are the material U.S. federal income tax consequences of the merger to me?\n999: A:\n1000: The\n1001: receipt of cash in exchange for shares of Mac-Gray common stock pursuant to the merger generally will be a taxable transaction for U.S. federal income\n1002: tax purposes. Generally, you will recognize gain or loss equal to the difference between the amount of cash you receive and the adjusted tax basis of your shares of Mac-Gray common stock. If you are a\n1003: U.S. holder (as defined in the section entitled \"Material U.S. Federal Income Tax Considerations with Respect to the Merger\"), you generally will be subject to U.S. federal income tax on any gain\n1004: recognized in connection with the merger. If you are a non-U.S. holder, you generally will not be subject to U.S. federal income tax on any gain recognized in connection with the merger unless you\n1005: have certain connections to the United States. However, the tax consequences of the merger to you will depend on your particular circumstances, and you should consult your own tax advisors to\n1006: determine how the merger will affect you. For a more detailed summary of the tax consequences of the merger, see \"Material U.S. Federal Income Tax Considerations with Respect to the Merger\" beginning\n1007: on page 82 of this proxy statement.\n1008: 17\n1009: \n1010: <<<PAGE 24 folio=18>>>\n1011: Table of Contents\n1012: Q:\n1013: Am I entitled to appraisal rights?\n1014: A:\n1015: If\n1016: the merger is completed, Mac-Gray stockholders who do not vote in favor of the adoption of the merger agreement will have the right to seek appraisal of\n1017: the fair value of their shares of Mac-Gray common stock, as determined by the Delaware Court of Chancery, but only if they properly perfect appraisal rights under Delaware law, as described in the\n1018: section entitled \"Appraisal Rights\" beginning on page 85 of this proxy statement. A copy of the full text of Section 262 of the DGCL is included as Annex C to this proxy statement.\n1019: Failure to strictly comply with the procedures set forth in Section 262 of the DGCL will result in the loss of appraisal rights.\n1020: Q:\n1021: Should I send in my stock certificates now?\n1022: A:\n1023: No.\n1024: After the merger is completed, you will be sent a letter of transmittal with detailed written instructions for exchanging your shares of Mac-Gray common\n1025: stock for the merger consideration. If your shares are held in \"street name\" by your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee, you will receive instructions from your brokerage firm, bank, trust\n1026: or other nominee as to how to effect the surrender of your \"street name\" shares in exchange for the merger consideration. PLEASE DO NOT SEND IN YOUR STOCK CERTIFICATES\n1027: NOW .\n1028: Q:\n1029: What happens if I sell my shares of Mac-Gray common stock before the special\n1030: meeting?\n1031: A:\n1032: The\n1033: record date for stockholders entitled to vote at the special meeting is earlier than the date of the special meeting and the expected closing date of the\n1034: merger. If you transfer your shares of Mac-Gray common stock after the record date but before the special meeting, you will, unless special arrangements are made, retain your right to vote at the\n1035: special meeting but will transfer the right to receive the merger consideration to the person to whom you transfer your shares.\n1036: In\n1037: addition, if you sell your shares prior to the special meeting or prior to the effective time of the merger, you will not be eligible to exercise your appraisal rights in respect of the merger. For\n1038: a more detailed discussion of your appraisal rights and the requirements for perfecting your appraisal rights, see \"Appraisal Rights\" beginning on page 85 and Annex C of this proxy statement.\n1039: Q:\n1040: What happens if the merger is not completed for any reason?\n1041: A:\n1042: If\n1043: the merger is not completed for any reason, Mac-Gray stockholders will not receive any payment for their shares in connection with the merger. Instead, we\n1044: will remain a stand-alone public company, and Mac-Gray common stock will continue to be listed and traded on the New York Stock Exchange. Under specified circumstances, we may be required to pay to\n1045: Parent the Mac-Gray termination fee associated with the transaction, as described below under \"The Merger Agreement—Termination Fees and Expenses\" beginning on page 79 of this proxy\n1046: statement.\n1047: Q:\n1048: Who can answer further questions?\n1049: A:\n1050: For\n1051: additional questions about the merger, assistance in submitting proxies or voting shares of Mac-Gray common stock, or to request additional copies of the\n1052: proxy statement or the enclosed proxy card, please contact our proxy solicitor at:\n1053: 18\n1054: \n1055: <<<PAGE 25 folio=19>>>\n1056: Table of Contents\n1057: 105 Madison Avenue\n1058: New York, NY 10016\n1059: proxy@mackenziepartners.com\n1060: (212) 929-5500 (Call Collect)\n1061: or\n1062: TOLL FREE 1-800-322-2885\n1063: If your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee holds your shares in \"street name,\" you should also call your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee for additional\n1064: information.\n1065: 19\n1066: \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1067]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /tmp/opencode/filing_pages.txt, lines 767-1066\n767: <<<PAGE 19 folio=13>>>\n768: Table of Contents\n769: QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS ABOUT THE MERGER\n770: AND THE SPECIAL MEETING\n771: The following questions and answers address briefly some questions you may have regarding the merger agreement,\n772: the merger and the special meeting. These questions and answers may not address all questions that may be important to you as a holder of shares of Mac-Gray common stock. For important additional\n773: information, please refer to the more detailed discussion contained elsewhere in this proxy statement, the annexes to this proxy statement and the documents referred to in this proxy statement,\n774: including the merger agreement.\n775: Q:\n776: What is the effect of the merger?\n777: A:\n778: Mac-Gray,\n779: the Buyer Entities and Merger Subsidiary have entered into the merger agreement. Subject to the terms and conditions of the merger agreement,\n780: Parent will acquire Mac-Gray through the merger of Merger Subsidiary, a wholly-owned subsidiary of Parent, with and into Mac-Gray. Mac-Gray will be the surviving corporation in the merger and will\n781: continue as a wholly-owned subsidiary of Parent.\n782: Q:\n783: What will happen to my Mac-Gray stock as a result of the merger?\n784: A:\n785: If\n786: the merger is completed, each share of Mac-Gray common stock that you hold at the effective time of the merger will be converted into the right to receive\n787: $21.25 in cash, without interest, less any applicable withholding taxes. This conversion does not apply to shares of Mac-Gray common stock held by any Mac-Gray stockholders who have properly\n788: perfected, and not withdrawn or lost, their appraisal rights under Delaware law as more fully described below. The closing sale price of a share of Mac-Gray common stock on the New York Stock Exchange\n789: on November 27, 2013 was $21.30. You are encouraged to obtain current market quotations for Mac-Gray common stock in connection with voting your shares.\n790: Q:\n791: What will happen to Mac-Gray generally as a result of the merger?\n792: A:\n793: If\n794: the merger is completed, Mac-Gray will cease to be an independent public company and will be wholly owned by Parent. As a result, you will no longer have\n795: any ownership interest in Mac-Gray. Upon completion of the merger, shares of Mac-Gray common stock will no longer be listed on the New York Stock Exchange, or any other stock exchange or quotation\n796: system. In addition, following the completion of the merger, the registration of Mac-Gray common stock and our reporting obligations under the Exchange Act will be terminated and there will be no\n797: further public market for shares of Mac-Gray common stock.\n798: Q:\n799: How does the merger consideration compare to the market price of Mac-Gray common\n800: stock?\n801: A:\n802: The\n803: merger consideration of $21.25 per share to be received by Mac-Gray stockholders represents:\n804: •\n805: an approximately 36% premium over the high of $15.61 per share for the 52-week period ending October 11, 2013; and\n806: •\n807: an approximately 44% premium to the $14.72 closing price of our common stock on October 11, 2013 (the last trading\n808: day prior to the date our Board of Directors approved the merger agreement).\n809: The\n810: closing sale price of a share of Mac-Gray common stock on the New York Stock Exchange on November 27, 2013 was $21.30. You are encouraged to obtain current market quotations for Mac-Gray\n811: common stock in connection with voting your shares.\n812: 13\n813: \n814: <<<PAGE 20 folio=14>>>\n815: Table of Contents\n816: Q:\n817: When do you expect the merger to be completed?\n818: A:\n819: We\n820: currently expect the merger to be completed in January 2014. However, the merger is subject to various closing conditions, including Mac-Gray stockholder\n821: and regulatory approvals, and it is possible that the failure to timely meet these closing conditions or other factors outside of our control could delay the completion of the merger. We cannot assure\n822: you that we will complete the merger on this schedule or at all.\n823: Q:\n824: Why am I receiving this proxy statement?\n825: A:\n826: You\n827: are receiving this proxy statement because you were a stockholder of Mac-Gray as of November 27, 2013, the record date for the special meeting.\n828: The adoption of the merger agreement requires the affirmative vote of a majority of the outstanding shares of Mac-Gray common stock entitled to vote at the special meeting. A copy of the merger\n829: agreement is attached to this proxy statement as Annex A. Mac-Gray will submit the merger agreement to its stockholders for adoption at the special meeting described in this proxy statement.\n830: You should read the section entitled \"The Special Meeting\" beginning on page 20 of this proxy statement.\n831: Q:\n832: When and where will the special meeting of stockholders be held?\n833: A:\n834: The\n835: special meeting of Mac-Gray stockholders will be held on Wednesday, January 8, 2014 at 10:00 a.m., local time, at the Goodwin Procter LLP Conference Center, Second Floor, Exchange Place, 53 State\n836: Street, Boston, Massachusetts 02109.\n837: Q:\n838: What are the proposals that will be voted on at the special meeting?\n839: A:\n840: You\n841: will be asked to consider and vote on (1) a proposal to adopt the merger agreement with the Buyer Entities and Merger Subsidiary, (2) an\n842: advisory (non-binding) proposal to approve the \"golden parachute\" compensation payable or that could become payable to the named executive officers of Mac-Gray in connection with the merger pursuant\n843: to the terms of the merger agreement and pre-existing severance arrangements and (3) a proposal to approve one or more adjournments or postponements of the special meeting, if necessary, to\n844: solicit additional proxies if Mac-Gray has not obtained sufficient affirmative stockholder votes to adopt the merger agreement.\n845: Q:\n846: How does the Mac-Gray Board of Directors recommend that I vote on the proposals?\n847: A:\n848: The\n849: Mac-Gray Board of Directors (upon the unanimous recommendation of the Special Committee) unanimously approved the merger agreement and determined that\n850: the merger and the other transactions contemplated by the merger agreement are advisable and in the best interests of Mac-Gray and our stockholders and unanimously recommends that you vote\n851: \" FOR \" the proposal to adopt the merger agreement. You should read the section entitled \"The Merger—Reasons for the Merger and Recommendation\n852: of our Board of Directors\" beginning on page 42 of this proxy statement. The Mac-Gray Board of Directors also unanimously recommends that you vote \" FOR \"\n853: the proposal to approve, on an advisory (non-binding) basis, the \"golden parachute\" compensation payable or that could become payable to the named executive officers of Mac-Gray in connection with the\n854: merger the terms of the merger agreement and pursuant to pre-existing severance arrangements and \" FOR \" the adjournment proposal.\n855: Q:\n856: Why am I being asked to cast an advisory (non-binding) vote to approve the \"golden parachute\" compensation payable or that could\n857: become payable to certain Mac-Gray officers in connection with the merger pursuant to the terms of the merger agreement and pre-existing severance\n858: arrangements?\n859: A:\n860: The\n861: SEC, in accordance with the Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act of 2010, adopted rules that require Mac-Gray to seek an advisory\n862: (non-binding) vote with respect to certain payments that may be made to Mac-Gray's named executive officers in connection with the merger.\n863: 14\n864: \n865: <<<PAGE 21 folio=15>>>\n866: Table of Contents\n867: Q:\n868: What will happen if Mac-Gray shareholders do not approve the \"golden parachute\" compensation at the special\n869: meeting?\n870: A:\n871: Approval\n872: of the \"golden parachute\" compensation payable or that could become payable to Mac-Gray's named executive officers in connection with the merger\n873: pursuant to the terms of the merger agreement and pre-existing severance arrangements is not a condition to completion of the merger. The vote with respect to the \"golden parachute\" compensation is an\n874: advisory vote and will not be binding on Mac-Gray regardless of whether the merger agreement is approved. Therefore, regardless of whether stockholders approve the \"golden parachute\" compensation, if\n875: the merger is approved by the stockholders and completed, the \"golden parachute\" compensation will still be paid to Mac-Gray's named executive officers to the extent payable in accordance with the\n876: terms of the merger agreement and pre-existing compensation arrangements.\n877: Q:\n878: What vote of Mac-Gray stockholders is required to adopt the proposal regarding \"golden parachute\" compensation and the proposal to\n879: adjourn the special meeting, if necessary?\n880: A:\n881: A\n882: majority of the votes properly cast will be required to approve the advisory (non-binding) vote on the \"golden parachute\" compensation payable or that\n883: could become payable to the named executive officers of Mac-Gray in connection with the merger and the adjournment, if necessary, of the special meeting to solicit additional proxies in favor of the\n884: approval and adoption of the merger agreement. Because the \"golden parachute\" compensation proposal is advisory, it will not be binding upon the Mac-Gray Board of Directors regardless of whether the\n885: merger agreement is approved. Failure to vote, in person or by proxy, will have no effect on the approval of the \"golden parachute\" compensation or any adjournment proposal.\n886: Q:\n887: Who is entitled to attend and vote at the special meeting?\n888: A:\n889: The\n890: record date for the special meeting is November 27, 2013. If you own shares of Mac-Gray common stock as of the close of business on the record\n891: date, you are entitled to notice of, and to vote at, the special meeting or any adjournment or postponement of the special meeting. As of the record date, there were approximately 14,758,306 shares of\n892: Mac-Gray common stock issued and outstanding held collectively by approximately 104 stockholders of record.\n893: Q:\n894: How many votes are required to adopt the merger agreement?\n895: A:\n896: Under\n897: applicable law, we must receive the affirmative approval of the holders of a majority of the outstanding shares of Mac-Gray common stock entitled to\n898: vote at the special meeting in order to complete the merger. In connection with the execution of the merger agreement, certain stockholders of Mac-Gray entered into voting agreements agreeing to vote\n899: in favor of the adoption of the merger agreement. As of November 27, 2013, the record date for the special meeting, these stockholders collectively owned approximately 15% of the Mac-Gray\n900: common stock entitled to vote at the special meeting. If the merger agreement is terminated in accordance with its terms, these voting agreements will also terminate.\n901: Q:\n902: How are votes counted?\n903: A:\n904: Votes\n905: will be counted by the inspector of election appointed for the special meeting, who will separately count \"FOR\" and \"AGAINST\" votes and abstentions.\n906: The adoption of the merger agreement requires the affirmative vote of a majority of the outstanding shares of Mac-Gray common stock entitled to vote at the special meeting. If you abstain from voting,\n907: fail to cast your vote, in person or by proxy, or fail to give voting instructions to your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee, it will have the same effect as a vote against the proposal to\n908: adopt the merger agreement.\n909: 15\n910: \n911: <<<PAGE 22 folio=16>>>\n912: Table of Contents\n913: Q:\n914: What do I need to do now?\n915: A:\n916: After\n917: carefully reading and considering the information contained in this proxy statement, including the annexes and the other documents referred to in this\n918: proxy statement, please vote your shares as described below. You have one vote for each share of Mac-Gray common stock you own as of the record date.\n919: Q:\n920: How do I vote if I am a stockholder of record?\n921: A:\n922: You\n923: may vote:\n924: •\n925: by telephone by following the voting instructions printed on your proxy card;\n926: •\n927: by Internet by following the voting instructions printed on your proxy card;\n928: •\n929: by completing, signing and dating each proxy card you receive and returning it in the enclosed postage paid envelope; or\n930: •\n931: in person by appearing and casting your vote at the special meeting.\n932: If\n933: you are voting by telephone or via the Internet, your voting instructions must be received by the date and time indicated on the applicable proxy card(s).\n934: Voting\n935: via the Internet, by telephone or by mailing in your proxy card will not prevent you from voting in person at the special meeting. You are encouraged to submit a proxy by mail, via the Internet\n936: or by telephone even if you plan to attend the special meeting in person, to ensure that your shares of Mac-Gray common stock are present in person or represented at the special meeting.\n937: If\n938: you return a properly signed and dated proxy card but do not mark the box showing how you wish to vote, your shares will be voted \" FOR \" the proposal\n939: to adopt the merger agreement, \" FOR \" the proposal to approve, on an advisory (non-binding) basis, the \"golden parachute\" compensation payable or that\n940: could become payable to the named executive officers of Mac-Gray in connection with the merger pursuant to the terms of the merger agreement and pre-existing severance arrangements and\n941: \" FOR \" the proposal to adjourn the special meeting. With respect to any other matter that properly comes before the special meeting, shares present in\n942: person or represented by all proxies received by Mac-Gray will be voted with respect to such matter in accordance with the judgment of the persons named as attorneys-in-fact in the proxies.\n943: Q:\n944: How do I vote if my shares are held by my brokerage firm, bank, trust or other\n945: nominee?\n946: A:\n947: If\n948: your shares are held in a brokerage account or by another nominee, such as a bank or trust, then the brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee is\n949: considered to be the stockholder of record with respect to those shares. However, you still are considered to be the beneficial owner of those shares, with your shares being held in \"street name.\"\n950: \"Street name\" holders generally cannot vote their shares directly and must instead instruct the brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee how to vote their shares. Your brokerage firm, bank, trust\n951: or other nominee will only be permitted to vote your shares for you at the special meeting if you instruct it how to vote. Therefore, it is important that you promptly follow the directions provided\n952: by your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee regarding how to instruct them to vote your shares. If you wish to vote in person at the special meeting, you must bring a proxy from your\n953: brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee authorizing you to vote at the special meeting.\n954: In\n955: addition, because any shares you may hold in \"street name\" will be deemed to be held by a different stockholder than any shares you hold of record, shares held in \"street name\" will not be combined\n956: for voting purposes with shares you hold of record. To be sure your shares are voted,\n957: 16\n958: \n959: <<<PAGE 23 folio=17>>>\n960: Table of Contents\n961: you\n962: should instruct your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee to vote your shares. Shares held by a corporation or business entity must be voted by an authorized officer of the entity.\n963: Q:\n964: What does it mean if I receive more than one proxy card?\n965: A:\n966: If\n967: you receive more than one proxy card, it means that you hold shares that are registered in more than one account. For example, if you own your shares in\n968: various registered forms, such as jointly with your spouse, as trustee of a trust or as custodian for a minor, you will receive, and you will need to sign and return, a separate proxy card for those\n969: shares because they are held in a different form of record ownership. Therefore, to ensure that all of your shares are voted, you will need to sign and return each proxy card you receive or vote by\n970: telephone or via the Internet by using the different control number(s) on each proxy card.\n971: Q:\n972: May I change my vote after I have delivered my proxy?\n973: A:\n974: Yes.\n975: If you are the stockholder of record of Mac-Gray common stock, you have the right to change or revoke your proxy at any time before the vote being taken\n976: at the special meeting:\n977: •\n978: by delivering to Mac-Gray's corporate secretary a signed written notice of revocation bearing a date later than the date\n979: of the proxy, stating that the proxy is revoked;\n980: •\n981: by attending the special meeting and voting in person (your attendance at the meeting will not, by itself, revoke your\n982: proxy; you must vote in person at the meeting);\n983: •\n984: by signing and delivering a new proxy relating to the same shares of Mac-Gray common stock and bearing a later date; or\n985: •\n986: by submitting another proxy by telephone or via the Internet by the date and time indicated on the applicable proxy\n987: card(s).\n988: The\n989: latest dated completed proxy will be the one which counts. Written notices of revocation and other communications with respect to the revocation of any proxies should be addressed to:\n990: Mac-Gray\n991: Corporation\n992: 404 Wyman Street, Suite 400\n993: Waltham, Massachusetts 02451\n994: Attn: Corporate Secretary\n995: If\n996: you are a \"street name\" holder of Mac-Gray common stock, you should contact your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee to obtain instructions as to how to change or revoke your proxy.\n997: Q:\n998: What are the material U.S. federal income tax consequences of the merger to me?\n999: A:\n1000: The\n1001: receipt of cash in exchange for shares of Mac-Gray common stock pursuant to the merger generally will be a taxable transaction for U.S. federal income\n1002: tax purposes. Generally, you will recognize gain or loss equal to the difference between the amount of cash you receive and the adjusted tax basis of your shares of Mac-Gray common stock. If you are a\n1003: U.S. holder (as defined in the section entitled \"Material U.S. Federal Income Tax Considerations with Respect to the Merger\"), you generally will be subject to U.S. federal income tax on any gain\n1004: recognized in connection with the merger. If you are a non-U.S. holder, you generally will not be subject to U.S. federal income tax on any gain recognized in connection with the merger unless you\n1005: have certain connections to the United States. However, the tax consequences of the merger to you will depend on your particular circumstances, and you should consult your own tax advisors to\n1006: determine how the merger will affect you. For a more detailed summary of the tax consequences of the merger, see \"Material U.S. Federal Income Tax Considerations with Respect to the Merger\" beginning\n1007: on page 82 of this proxy statement.\n1008: 17\n1009: \n1010: <<<PAGE 24 folio=18>>>\n1011: Table of Contents\n1012: Q:\n1013: Am I entitled to appraisal rights?\n1014: A:\n1015: If\n1016: the merger is completed, Mac-Gray stockholders who do not vote in favor of the adoption of the merger agreement will have the right to seek appraisal of\n1017: the fair value of their shares of Mac-Gray common stock, as determined by the Delaware Court of Chancery, but only if they properly perfect appraisal rights under Delaware law, as described in the\n1018: section entitled \"Appraisal Rights\" beginning on page 85 of this proxy statement. A copy of the full text of Section 262 of the DGCL is included as Annex C to this proxy statement.\n1019: Failure to strictly comply with the procedures set forth in Section 262 of the DGCL will result in the loss of appraisal rights.\n1020: Q:\n1021: Should I send in my stock certificates now?\n1022: A:\n1023: No.\n1024: After the merger is completed, you will be sent a letter of transmittal with detailed written instructions for exchanging your shares of Mac-Gray common\n1025: stock for the merger consideration. If your shares are held in \"street name\" by your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee, you will receive instructions from your brokerage firm, bank, trust\n1026: or other nominee as to how to effect the surrender of your \"street name\" shares in exchange for the merger consideration. PLEASE DO NOT SEND IN YOUR STOCK CERTIFICATES\n1027: NOW .\n1028: Q:\n1029: What happens if I sell my shares of Mac-Gray common stock before the special\n1030: meeting?\n1031: A:\n1032: The\n1033: record date for stockholders entitled to vote at the special meeting is earlier than the date of the special meeting and the expected closing date of the\n1034: merger. If you transfer your shares of Mac-Gray common stock after the record date but before the special meeting, you will, unless special arrangements are made, retain your right to vote at the\n1035: special meeting but will transfer the right to receive the merger consideration to the person to whom you transfer your shares.\n1036: In\n1037: addition, if you sell your shares prior to the special meeting or prior to the effective time of the merger, you will not be eligible to exercise your appraisal rights in respect of the merger. For\n1038: a more detailed discussion of your appraisal rights and the requirements for perfecting your appraisal rights, see \"Appraisal Rights\" beginning on page 85 and Annex C of this proxy statement.\n1039: Q:\n1040: What happens if the merger is not completed for any reason?\n1041: A:\n1042: If\n1043: the merger is not completed for any reason, Mac-Gray stockholders will not receive any payment for their shares in connection with the merger. Instead, we\n1044: will remain a stand-alone public company, and Mac-Gray common stock will continue to be listed and traded on the New York Stock Exchange. Under specified circumstances, we may be required to pay to\n1045: Parent the Mac-Gray termination fee associated with the transaction, as described below under \"The Merger Agreement—Termination Fees and Expenses\" beginning on page 79 of this proxy\n1046: statement.\n1047: Q:\n1048: Who can answer further questions?\n1049: A:\n1050: For\n1051: additional questions about the merger, assistance in submitting proxies or voting shares of Mac-Gray common stock, or to request additional copies of the\n1052: proxy statement or the enclosed proxy card, please contact our proxy solicitor at:\n1053: 18\n1054: \n1055: <<<PAGE 25 folio=19>>>\n1056: Table of Contents\n1057: 105 Madison Avenue\n1058: New York, NY 10016\n1059: proxy@mackenziepartners.com\n1060: (212) 929-5500 (Call Collect)\n1061: or\n1062: TOLL FREE 1-800-322-2885\n1063: If your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee holds your shares in \"street name,\" you should also call your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other nominee for additional\n1064: information.\n1065: 19\n1066: \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1067]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 300, \"offset\": 767, \"path\": \"/tmp/opencode/filing_pages.txt\"}"},"time":{"start":1789824337309,"end":1789824337480}}}}
