{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789824351933,"sessionID":"ses_f46293753ffeU5Yf7iVUgOE0mm","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9d83e10001ZtGTKFJGawUi5E","sessionID":"ses_f46293753ffeU5Yf7iVUgOE0mm","messageID":"msg_0b9d834ba001mpAXkyUbwAaflo","type":"tool","id":"call_00_MhaiUtLrpLcEcjKfmywa8173","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":220,"offset":1894,"path":"/tmp/opencode/filing_pages.txt"},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/filing_pages.txt, lines 1894-2113\n1894: considered a number of factors, including the following factors:\n1895: •\n1896: information concerning our business, financial performance (both past and prospective) and our financial condition,\n1897: results of operations (both past and prospective), business and strategic objectives, as well as the risks and benefits of accomplishing those objectives;\n1898: •\n1899: opportunities if Mac-Gray were to remain independent, including the ability to improve profitability and EBITDA margins by\n1900: increasing scale and density through organic growth and/or strategic acquisitions as well as the ability to increase revenue through vend increases and the conversion from coin-operated technology to\n1901: card-based technology;\n1902: •\n1903: risks associated with executing and achieving Mac-Gray's short and long-term business and financial plans, including\n1904: Mac-Gray's ability to identify, acquire and successfully integrate laundry facilities management businesses, establish new and/or renew existing laundry facility management leases and penetrate\n1905: markets historically dominated by smaller local and regional operators, the impact of general economic and market trends on Mac-Gray's sales and the general risks of market conditions that could\n1906: reduce Mac-Gray's stock price and the risks involved if Mac-Gray was not able to execute on its strategic business plan, including the difficulties with increasing stock price without a significant or\n1907: transformative transaction, such as a major acquisition or restructuring;\n1908: •\n1909: the possible alternatives to the merger (including the possibility of continuing to operate as an independent entity and a\n1910: stock repurchase, and the perceived risks thereof), the range of possible benefits to our stockholders of those alternatives and the timing and the likelihood of accomplishing the goal of any of such\n1911: alternatives, and the Special Committee's assessment that the merger with CSC presented a superior opportunity to such alternatives for our stockholders;\n1912: •\n1913: the results of discussions with third parties, including Party A, Party B, Party C and other potential bidders, relating\n1914: to a possible business combination with or acquisition of Mac-Gray;\n1915: •\n1916: the process undertaken by our Board of Directors and the Special Committee, assisted by its legal and financial advisors,\n1917: in connection with pursuing a strategic transaction and the terms and conditions of the proposed merger, in each case in light of the current market dynamics in the industry;\n1918: •\n1919: current financial market conditions and historical market prices, volatility and trading information with respect to\n1920: Mac-Gray common stock;\n1921: •\n1922: the potential for obtaining a superior offer from an alternative purchaser in light of the other potential purchasers\n1923: previously identified and contacted by BofA Merrill Lynch at the instruction of the Special Committee, including Party A, Party B and Party C, and the risk of losing the proposed transaction with\n1924: CSC/Pamplona; and\n1925: •\n1926: the terms of the merger agreement, including the parties' representations, warranties and covenants, the certainty of\n1927: financing, the conditions to parties' respective obligations and the termination rights of the parties.\n1928: 42\n1929: \n1930: <<<PAGE 49 folio=43>>>\n1931: Table of Contents\n1932: In\n1933: the course of its deliberations, the Special Committee and our Board of Directors also considered, among other things, the following positive\n1934: factors:\n1935: •\n1936: the value of the consideration to be received by our stockholders in the merger pursuant to the merger agreement;\n1937: •\n1938: the fact that the $21.25 per share to be paid as the consideration in the merger represents a premium of approximately 36%\n1939: over the high of $15.61 per share for the 52-week period ending October 11, 2013 and a premium of approximately 44% over the $14.72 closing trading price for the shares of our common stock on\n1940: the NYSE on October 11, 2013, the last trading day prior to the date our Board of Directors approved the merger agreement;\n1941: •\n1942: the multiple of our EBITDA and revenue represented by the $21.25 per share purchase price relative to EBITDA and revenue\n1943: multiples represented by the consideration paid in comparable precedent transactions;\n1944: •\n1945: the opinion of BofA Merrill Lynch, dated October 14, 2013, to the Special Committee as to the fairness, from a\n1946: financial point of view and as of the date of the opinion, of the consideration to be received by holders of Mac-Gray common stock, as more fully described below in the section entitled \"Opinion of\n1947: Mac-Gray's Financial Advisor\";\n1948: •\n1949: the likelihood that the proposed acquisition would be consummated, in light of the experience, reputation and financial\n1950: capabilities of CSC/Pamplona;\n1951: •\n1952: the Pamplona commitment letter, pursuant to which Pamplona has committed to fund up to $594 million to the Buyer\n1953: Entities to fund the payment of the merger consideration and other amounts payable pursuant to the merger agreement or to satisfy any damages (up to $50 million) awarded to Mac-Gray in certain\n1954: circumstances involving litigation brought pursuant to the merger agreement;\n1955: •\n1956: CSC and Merger Subsidiary representations to us in the merger agreement that they will have adequate capital resources to\n1957: pay the merger consideration and other amounts to consummate the merger, taking into account the proceeds from the Pamplona commitment letter;\n1958: •\n1959: the form and timing of merger consideration, consisting solely of cash at closing, which provides certainty of value to\n1960: our stockholders;\n1961: •\n1962: the process through which Mac-Gray, with the assistance of BofA Merrill Lynch, at the instruction of the Special\n1963: Committee, engaged in or sought to engage in discussions with other companies believed to be the most likely candidates to pursue a business combination with or acquisition of Mac-Gray;\n1964: •\n1965: the belief of the Special Committee and the Mac-Gray Board of Directors that, after extensive negotiations with\n1966: CSC/Pamplona and their representatives, we have obtained the highest price per share that CSC/Pamplona are willing to pay and the highest price obtainable on the date of signing of the merger\n1967: agreement;\n1968: •\n1969: the merger agreement, subject to the limitations and requirements contained in the agreement, provides our Board of\n1970: Directors (upon the recommendation of the Special Committee) with flexibility to furnish information to and conduct negotiations with third parties in certain circumstances and, upon payment to CSC of\n1971: the Mac-Gray termination fee $11 million (which was heavily negotiated and which our Board of Directors believes is reasonable under the circumstances) to terminate the merger agreement, to\n1972: accept a superior offer;\n1973: •\n1974: the fact that the confidentiality and standstill agreements entered into with potential bidders contain sunset provisions\n1975: for the standstill restrictions, which permits these potential bidders to submit unsolicited offers to acquire Mac-Gray;\n1976: 43\n1977: \n1978: <<<PAGE 50 folio=44>>>\n1979: Table of Contents\n1980: •\n1981: the other terms and conditions of the merger agreement and the Pamplona commitment letter, including among other things\n1982: the size of the Mac-Gray termination fee and the circumstances when that fee may be payable; the size of the CSC reverse termination fee and the circumstances when that fee may be payable; the scope\n1983: of the post-signing covenants; the limited number and nature of the conditions to the Buyer Entities' obligation to complete the merger, including (but not limited to) the absence of a financing\n1984: condition and the adequacy of CSC's capital resources to pay the merger consideration; the fact that Pamplona will be, subject to certain conditions and limitations, responsible to fund the merger\n1985: consideration to cause the closing or to pay certain damages if the closing does not occur; and the definition of \"material adverse effect\" (including the exceptions for what constitutes a material\n1986: adverse effect for purposes of the merger agreement); and\n1987: •\n1988: the voting agreements terminate in the event that we terminate the merger agreement which permits those stockholders to\n1989: support a transaction involving a superior offer.\n1990: In\n1991: the course of its deliberations, the Special Committee and our Board of Directors also considered, among other things, the following negative\n1992: factors:\n1993: •\n1994: the potential loss of customer, supplier or other commercial relationships of Mac-Gray as a result of the merger and/or\n1995: the announcement of the merger or other potential disruption to customer, supplier, vendor or other commercial relationships important to us as a result of the merger and/or the announcement of the\n1996: merger;\n1997: •\n1998: the possibility that the merger will not be consummated and the potential negative effect of the public announcement of\n1999: the merger on our sales, operating results and stock price and our ability to retain employees;\n2000: •\n2001: the fact that, after the merger, our stockholders would not participate in any future growth potential or benefit from any\n2002: future increase in our value;\n2003: •\n2004: the conditions to CSC's obligation to complete the merger and the right of CSC to terminate the merger agreement under\n2005: certain circumstances;\n2006: •\n2007: the possibility that we may be obligated to pay CSC the Mac-Gray termination fee of $11 million if the merger\n2008: agreement is terminated under certain circumstances;\n2009: •\n2010: the fact that the merger consideration consists of cash and will therefore be taxable to our stockholders for U.S. federal\n2011: income tax purposes;\n2012: •\n2013: the restrictions on our ability to solicit or engage in discussions or negotiations regarding alternative business\n2014: combination transactions, subject to specified exceptions, and the requirement that we pay the Mac-Gray termination fee of $11 million in order to accept a superior acquisition proposal, which\n2015: may discourage a competing proposal to acquire us that may be more advantageous to our stockholders;\n2016: •\n2017: the restrictions on the conduct of our business prior to the completion of the merger, requiring us to conduct our\n2018: business in the ordinary course, subject to specific limitations, which may delay or prevent us from undertaking business opportunities that may arise pending completion of the merger;\n2019: •\n2020: the risk of diverting management's focus and resources from other strategic opportunities and from operational matters\n2021: while working to implement the merger, and the possibility of other management and employee disruption associated with the merger, including the possible loss of employees, including key members of\n2022: management; and\n2023: 44\n2024: \n2025: <<<PAGE 51 folio=45>>>\n2026: Table of Contents\n2027: •\n2028: the interests that certain of our directors and executive officers may have with respect to the merger, in addition to\n2029: their interests as stockholders of Mac-Gray generally, as described in \"The Merger—Interests of Our Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger.\"\n2030: The\n2031: preceding discussion of the information and factors considered by the Special Committee and our Board of Directors is not, and is not intended to be, exhaustive. In light of the variety of factors\n2032: considered in connection with their evaluation of the merger and the complexity of these matters, the Special Committee and our Board of Directors did not find it practicable to, and did not, quantify\n2033: or otherwise attempt to assign relative weights to the various factors considered in reaching its determination. In addition, the Special Committee and our Board of Directors did not undertake to make\n2034: any specific determination as to whether any particular factor, or any aspect of any particular factor, was favorable or unfavorable to the ultimate determination of the Special Committee and our\n2035: Board of Directors, but rather the Special Committee and our Board of Directors conducted an overall analysis of the factors described above, including discussions with and questioning of our senior\n2036: management, Goodwin Procter and BofA Merrill Lynch.\n2037: Board of Directors Recommendation\n2038: After careful consideration and based upon the unanimous recommendation of the Special Committee, our Board of Directors has\n2039: unanimously approved the merger agreement and declared that the merger agreement, the merger and the other transactions contemplated by the merger agreement are advisable and in the best interests of\n2040: Mac-Gray stockholders. Accordingly, our Board of Directors unanimously recommends that our stockholders approve the proposal to adopt the merger agreement and that you vote\n2041: \"FOR\" the proposal to adopt the merger agreement at the special meeting.\n2042: Opinion of Mac-Gray's Financial Advisor\n2043: Mac-Gray has retained BofA Merrill Lynch to act as Mac-Gray's financial advisor in connection with the merger. BofA Merrill Lynch is an\n2044: internationally recognized investment banking firm which is regularly engaged in the valuation of businesses and securities in connection with mergers and acquisitions, negotiated underwritings,\n2045: secondary distributions of listed and unlisted securities, private placements and valuations for corporate and other purposes. Mac-Gray selected BofA Merrill Lynch to act as Mac-Gray's financial\n2046: advisor in connection with the merger on the basis of BofA Merrill Lynch's experience in transactions similar to the merger, its reputation in the investment community and its familiarity with the\n2047: laundry facilities management industry and Mac-Gray and its business.\n2048: On\n2049: October 14, 2013, at a meeting of the Special Committee held to evaluate the merger, BofA Merrill Lynch delivered to the Special Committee an oral opinion, which was confirmed\n2050: by delivery of a written opinion dated October 14, 2013, to the effect that, as of the date of the opinion and based on and subject to various assumptions and limitations described in its\n2051: opinion, the merger consideration to be received by holders of Mac-Gray common stock was fair, from a financial point of view, to such holders.\n2052: The full text of BofA Merrill Lynch's written opinion to the Special Committee, which describes, among other things, the assumptions made, procedures followed,\n2053: factors considered and limitations on the review undertaken, is attached as Annex B to this document and is incorporated by reference herein in its entirety. The following summary of BofA\n2054: Merrill Lynch's opinion is qualified in its entirety by reference to the full text of the opinion. BofA Merrill Lynch delivered its opinion to the Special Committee for the benefit and use of the\n2055: Special Committee (in its capacity as such) in connection with and for purposes of its evaluation of the merger consideration from a financial point of view. BofA Merrill Lynch's opinion does not\n2056: address any other aspect of the merger and no opinion or view\n2057: was expressed as to the relative merits of the merger in comparison to other strategies or transactions that might be available to Mac-Gray or in which Mac-Gray might engage or as to the\n2058: 45\n2059: \n2060: <<<PAGE 52 folio=46>>>\n2061: Table of Contents\n2062: underlying business decision of Mac-Gray to proceed with or effect the merger. BofA Merrill Lynch's opinion does not address any other aspect of the merger and does not constitute a recommendation to\n2063: any stockholder as to how to vote or act in connection with the proposed merger or any related matter.\n2064: In\n2065: connection with rendering its opinion, BofA Merrill Lynch:\n2066: (1)\n2067: reviewed\n2068: certain publicly available business and financial information relating to Mac-Gray;\n2069: (2)\n2070: reviewed\n2071: certain internal financial and operating information with respect to the business, operations and prospects of Mac-Gray furnished to or discussed\n2072: with BofA Merrill Lynch by the management of Mac-Gray, including certain financial forecasts relating to Mac-Gray prepared by the management of Mac-Gray, referred to herein as the Projections and\n2073: which are described under the caption \"—Projected Financial Information\";\n2074: (3)\n2075: discussed\n2076: the past and current business, operations, financial condition and prospects of Mac-Gray with members of senior management of Mac-Gray;\n2077: (4)\n2078: reviewed\n2079: the trading history for Mac-Gray common stock and a comparison of that trading history with the trading histories of other companies BofA Merrill\n2080: Lynch deemed relevant;\n2081: (5)\n2082: compared\n2083: certain financial and stock market information of Mac-Gray with similar information of other companies BofA Merrill Lynch deemed relevant;\n2084: (6)\n2085: compared\n2086: certain financial terms of the merger to financial terms, to the extent publicly available, of other transactions BofA Merrill Lynch deemed\n2087: relevant;\n2088: (7)\n2089: considered\n2090: the results of BofA Merrill Lynch's efforts on behalf of Mac-Gray to solicit, at the direction of the Special Committee, indications of interest\n2091: and definitive proposals from third parties with respect to a possible acquisition of Mac-Gray;\n2092: (8)\n2093: reviewed\n2094: a draft of the merger agreement and certain related documents;\n2095: (9)\n2096: reviewed\n2097: (i) the voting agreement by and between Parent and Stewart MacDonald, Jr., dated as of September 27, 2013, (ii) the voting\n2098: agreement by and between Parent and Cynthia Doggett, dated as of September 27, 2013, (iii) the voting agreement by and between Parent and The Stewart G. MacDonald, Jr. 2011 Trust, dated\n2099: as of September 27, 2013 and (iv) a draft of the voting agreement by and between Parent on the one hand and Moab Capital Partners, LLC, a Delaware limited liability company and\n2100: Moab Partners, LP, a Delaware limited partnership, on the other hand; and\n2101: (10)\n2102: performed\n2103: such other analyses and studies and considered such other information and factors as BofA Merrill Lynch deemed appropriate.\n2104: In\n2105: arriving at its opinion, BofA Merrill Lynch assumed and relied upon, without independent verification, the accuracy and completeness of the financial and other information and data\n2106: publicly available or provided to or otherwise reviewed by or discussed with it and relied upon the assurances of the management of Mac-Gray that management was not aware of any facts or circumstances\n2107: that would make such information or data inaccurate or misleading in any material respect. With respect to the Projections, BofA Merrill Lynch was advised by Mac-Gray, and assumed, that they were\n2108: reasonably prepared on bases reflecting the best currently available estimates and good faith judgments of the management of Mac-Gray as to the future financial performance 